## AI Assistant Guidelines

### When Making Changes
1. **Preserve Windows Forms patterns** - Use existing helper functions from lib.ps1
2. **Maintain data persistence** - Update both GUI and JSON save logic
3. **Test save/load cycles** - Ensure $Global:isLoading flag prevents corruption
4. **FFmpeg commands** - Always use -y flag for overwrite, validate exit codes
5. **User feedback** - Show MessageBox for errors, write-host for progress
6. **Debounced saves** - Use Save-GUIData for user input, Save-GUIDataImmediate for critical operations

### When Adding Features
1. **Follow PowerShell conventions** - Verb-Noun functions, [CmdletBinding()]
2. **Use FFmpegManager** - Always call Ensure-FFmpeg before using FFmpeg
3. **Update JSON schema** - Add new fields to archive format if needed
4. **GUI layout** - Use create-* functions, maintain consistent spacing
5. **Error handling** - Return $false on failure, show user-friendly messages

### When Debugging
1. **Check console output** - Many debug messages use write-host
2. **Verify FFmpeg commands** - Commands are printed before execution
3. **Check JSON files** - Archive files show exact saved state
4. **Test with/without GPU** - NVIDIA encoding may fail on non-NVIDIA systems
5. **PowerShell version** - Ensure running in PowerShell 5.1, not 7+

### Code Quality Standards
- **No hardcoded paths** - Use $PSScriptRoot for relative paths
- **Validate user input** - Check time ranges, file existence
- **Clean up temporary files** - Always remove temp files in process end
- **User confirmation** - Ask before destructive operations (file deletion)
- **Consistent indentation** - Use 4 spaces (PowerShell standard)

## FFmpeg Command Reference

### Get Video Duration
```powershell
ffprobe -v quiet -show_entries format=duration -of default=noprint_wrappers=1:nokey=1 video.mp4
```

### Concatenate Videos (Copy codec)
```powershell
ffmpeg -f concat -safe 0 -i filelist.txt -c copy output.mp4 -y
```

### Complex Filter (Trim & Concat)
```powershell
ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -filter_complex "[0]atrim=3:12,asetpts=PTS-STARTPTS[ap1],[0]trim=3:12,setpts=PTS-STARTPTS[p1],[p1][ap1]concat=n=1:v=1:a=1[out][aout]" -map "[out]" -map "[aout]" output.mp4 -y
```

### GPU Encoding (NVIDIA)
```powershell
-c:v h264_nvenc -preset p4 -b:v 20M -hwaccel cuda -hwaccel_output_format cuda
```

### CPU Encoding (Fallback)
```powershell
-c:v libx264 -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p -b:v 4000k
```

## Testing Checklist
When modifying the codebase, test:
- [ ] Single video selection works
- [ ] Multi-video concatenation prompts correctly
- [ ] Highlights save automatically (check archive/ folder)
- [ ] Highlights load on reopening same video
- [ ] Summary length auto-adjusts when highlights exceed 70%
- [ ] RUN button generates video successfully
- [ ] End screen appears (if EndScreenBackground.JPG exists)
- [ ] Comments appear on video as text overlays
- [ ] Delete button removes highlights and re-saves
- [ ] FFmpeg auto-downloads on first run
- [ ] Video output is playable and correct duration

## Common User Scenarios

### Scenario 1: First Time User
1. Clone repo and run GUI.ps1
2. FFmpeg downloads automatically (~100MB)
3. Select video file
4. Click + button to add highlights
5. Set start time, end time auto-fills (+5s)
6. Add comment for overlay text
7. Click RUN to generate summary

### Scenario 2: Resume Previous Session
1. Run GUI.ps1
2. Select same video file
3. GUI auto-loads highlights from archive/{video}.json
4. Modify highlights or add new ones
5. Click RUN to regenerate

### Scenario 3: Concatenate Split Videos
1. Run GUI.ps1
2. Select multiple video files (Ctrl+Click)
3. Dialog asks to concatenate
4. Tool merges files (fast, copy codec)
5. Asks to delete originals after verification
6. Proceeds with merged video

## Variable Naming Conventions
- `$TP*` - TimePicker controls
- `$TB*` - TextBox controls
- `$BT*` - Button controls
- `$LB*` - Label controls
- `$GP*` - GroupBox containers
- `$NB*` - NumericUpDown controls
- `$CB*` - Checkbox controls

## Key Insight: 70% Rule
The tool maintains a 70% rule: manual highlights can occupy up to 70% of the target summary length. The remaining 30% is auto-filled with distributed clips from the full video. If highlights exceed 70%, the summary length auto-extends.

This creates a balanced mix of user-selected highlights and algorithm-chosen filler content for smooth pacing.

